course: dictionary
lesson: 11525
title: Allocation: sharing out and the share
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An allocation is something that is shared out or given out for a purpose. Allocation can mean the act of sharing: «The allocation of seats took ten minutes.» Here allocation is the sharing itself, the work of deciding who gets which seat.
explain: Allocation can also mean the amount that one person or one use receives. «Our allocation of tickets is 20.» Here allocation means the number of tickets our group gets. Ask yourself: is someone doing the sharing, or is this the share that was given?
explain: Clue words often sit next to allocation: tickets, seats, budget, time, lockers. «The school's budget allocation for books went up this year.» A budget is money planned for spending, so the allocation is the money set aside for books.
fact: Allocation means sharing out or giving out something for a purpose.
fact: An allocation can also be the amount that one person or one use receives.
fact: «The allocation of lockers» means the sharing out of lockers to pupils.
fact: Common phrases are budget allocation, ticket allocation and time allocation.
fact: Allocation is a formal, stiffer word for share, so in everyday talk you might say share instead.
exercise: «The teacher made the allocation of classrooms before term began.» What does allocation mean here? | * The sharing out of classrooms | A list of lunch times | A room with a broken window | A school trip
exercise: «Our allocation of tickets is 20.» What does allocation mean here? | * The number of tickets we are given | A long queue | The people who sold the tickets | The price of each ticket
exercise: Which phrase means the money planned for spending on a project? | * Budget allocation | Sports day | Dinner hall | Ticket line
test: «The coach gave each team an allocation of ten balls.» What is the allocation here? | A new pitch | The game that the teams play | * The ten balls given to each team | The coach's whistle
test: «Time allocation for maths is one hour a day.» What does time allocation mean? | The lesson where children learn to count | * The amount of time planned for maths | A test at the end of the week | The clock on the classroom wall
exam: «The head teacher's allocation of tasks was fair.» Which word is closest in meaning to allocation in this sentence? | Mistake | Ending | Question | * Share
